## Setting Up

We will do this once, on Friday, after your phone arrives. These are not
instructions; they're just reminders for me.

1. Switch from old phone to new phone on Verizon
2. Import contacts from old phone
3. Set up voice recognition ([train the phone](https://support.google.com/assistant/answer/9071681?hl=en&co=GENIE.Platform%3DAndroid#zippy=%2Cteach-google-assistant-your-voice-again))


## Side button, volume, swiping

There are several gestures and buttons that can control the phone. Gestures are
things like swiping with your finger on the screen. You should try these a bunch
when you first get the phone so that you get a good feel for how they work. Even
if you mostly control the phone by talking to it, it is useful to be comfortable
with the physical controls.

When you swipe from an edge, you should start at the edge of the screen and drag
your finger toward the opposite edge. You don't need to press hard, and it
should be about as fast as a flick, like you're trying to swipe off a bit of
dust.

- Swipe from the *bottom* edge toward the top will *always* return you to your
  _home screen_.
- Swipe from the *left* edge will go "back", to the previous screen
- Swipe from the *right* edge will go "forward", to the next screen -- if there
  is one
- Swipe from the *top* edge shows you controls and messages -- swipe *again*
  from the top will expand the controls.

The _home screen_ is the screen that has your apps and contacts. If you ever get
lost in the phone, *swipe up* from the bottom should get you back to the start.

The left and right swipes are for backing up or moving forward. If you (for
example) go to your contacts and press one of the buttons at the bottom, you can
"back up" to the previous screen by swiping from the left to the right.

The top-swipe shows you the phone controls. There are many buttons here, but
also message people have sent you and other notifications. These control buttons
allow you to, for example, turn on airplane mode, turn on "do not distrub" mode,
and so on. When a button is colored blue, it is on; when it is sort of gray-ish,
it is off. Just remember: if you turn on something like airplane mode, you will
not receive any calls until you turn it off again!


## How to talk to the phone

When you say "Hey Google," it will start listening for commands. The phone
understands several commands, and will tell you if it doesn't understand you.
You must **always** start with "Hey Google."

Some examples are:

- "Hey Google, what time does lifesource open?"
- "Hey Google, what is the capital of south dakota?"
- "Hey Google, call Astrud"
- "Hey Google, text Jodi"
- "Hey Google, text Bob that I will call her tomorrow"
- "Hey Google, set an alarm for 3 pm"
- "Hey Google, remind me to call Jodi at 10 am tomorrow"
- "Hey Google, show me messages from Sean"
- "Hey Google, send an email to Astrud, subject How is Sarah's cold, message I
  hope she's feeling better, full stop"
- "Hey Google, listen to my voice mail"
- "Hey Google, play Ludovico Einaudi Elements"
- "Hey Google, take a picture"
- "Hey Google, show me pictures of the Golden Gate Bridge"
- "Hey Google, navigate to Grocery Store"
- "Hey Google, what time is it?"
- "Hey Google, what will the weather be like tomorrow?"
- "Hey Google, what time is it in Minneapolis?"
- "Hey Google, how many pounds is 14 kilograms?"
- "Hey Google, what is 132 divided by 13?"
- "Hey Google, turn on airplane mode" (and "Hey Google, turn off airplane mode")
- "Hey Google, turn on the flashlight" (and "Hey Google, turn off the flashlight")


## Apps, taking pictures

There are many programs (apps) on the phone, but I've selected a few for easy
access. I can add or remove apps to your home screen, but the more you add, the
more you have to search through.

: Phone

  This is for calling people

: Messages

  This sends text messages

: Calculator

  It's a simple calculator, like the physical ones we used to have

: Clock

  This can show the time, a stopwatch, a countdown timer, and set alarms.

: Camera

  For taking pictures

: Photos

  For viewing and sharing pictures



## Remote access

There is an app called RustDesk which will allow me to look at and control your
phone. You have to turn it on whenever you restart (turn off/on) the phone. When
I connect, you get a notification.

To start the app,

- go to your home screen (swipe up from the bottom) and click "All Apps" at the
  bottom of the screen.

  - If the bottom says "All Contacts," then press the "Apps" word near the top.

- Scroll down until you find RustDesk. The apps are in alphabetical order
- Tap on RustDesk
- When it opens, find the "Share Screen" text at the bottom of the screen and
  tap it
- It will say "Service is not running". Below this is a button that says "Start
  Service". Tap this.
- A "Warning" message will pop up with two buttons "Cancel" and "OK". Tap "OK."
- _Another_ message will pop up saying "Start recording or casting with
  RustDesk?"  Tap "Start now."
- The phone will make a noise and might show another small message.
- Swipe up from the bottom of the screen to return to your home screen.

It's ready to go. RustDesk will stay running until you turn it off, or restart
the phone.

RustDesk will let me change the settings, fix things, or just explain things you
see on the phone if you want or need me to.


## Charging

You can charge the phone two ways:

- Wireless charging
- Plug-in

For wireless charging, place the phone on the charging disk, on its back (with
the screen up). Place it so that it is centered on the disk, with the middle of
the phone in the center of the disk.

For plug-in, use a USB-C cable. Plug one end into a wall wart, and the other
into the phone.

If you travel to visit Astrud, Jodi, or Bob, they will all have spare
USB-C cables and chargers that you can use, so you don't need to bring any.
